.Dd November 10, 2026
.Dt SYSFS 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm sysfs
.Nd the devices, input devices, display devices, network interfaces, processors, firmware, clock, backlights and sleep states under /sys
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel mounts a synthetic, read-only file system at
.Pa /sys ,
as Linux mounts sysfs there, with the parts of Linux's tree that
BusyBox's
.Ic lspci
and
.Ic lsusb
read, each PCI function and each running USB device and what it says of
itself in its
.Pa uevent ,
a PCI function's identity and configuration space,
the input devices under
.Pa /sys/class/input ,
which udev and libinput read, each below the i8042 port or the USB
interface it came from, with the uevents that announce them,
the display devices under
.Pa /sys/class/drm ,
which libdrm, Mesa and libudev read to find each card, with the
uevents that announce them,
the network interfaces under
.Pa /sys/class/net ,
which scripts read for an interface's address, state and counters, the
real-time clock under
.Pa /sys/class/rtc ,
the panel's backlights under
.Pa /sys/class/backlight ,
and the sleep states under
.Pa /sys/power .
Any user may read it; root writes the files of mode 0644.
A write of more than 31 bytes to one of the fixed files is
.Er EINVAL
and changes nothing; a
.Pa uevent
takes up to 256 bytes, and a driver's own file up to 4096.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:BB:DD.F
A symbolic link for each PCI function, named by its bus, device and
function in lowercase hexadecimal, to
.Pa ../../../devices/pci0000:00/0000:BB:DD.F .
The functions are listed in the order of bus, device and function.
.It Pa /sys/devices/pci0000:00/0000:BB:DD.F/uevent
The function's variables, one
.Ql NAME=value
a line:
.Ql PCI_CLASS ,
the 24-bit class code in uppercase hexadecimal, at least four digits;
.Ql PCI_ID
and
.Ql PCI_SUBSYS_ID ,
the vendor and device of the function and of its subsystem, as
.Ql VVVV:DDDD ;
.Ql PCI_SLOT_NAME ,
the directory's name; and
.Ql MODALIAS ,
as Linux writes it.
The subsystem is 0000:0000 for a bridge.
A write of an action sends that uevent (see
.Sx UEVENTS ) .
.It Pa /sys/devices/pci0000:00/0000:BB:DD.F/{vendor,device,subsystem_vendor,subsystem_device,class,revision}
The function's identity, as Linux's pci-sysfs.c writes it:
.Ql 0x
and four lowercase hexadecimal digits for the IDs, six for the class and
two for the revision, and a newline.
.It Pa /sys/devices/pci0000:00/0000:BB:DD.F/boot_vga
On a VGA function alone,
.Ql 1
for the first VGA function in bus order that decodes memory and I/O, the
one the firmware's display ran on, as Linux's VGA arbiter takes it, and
.Ql 0
for the others.
.It Pa /sys/devices/pci0000:00/0000:BB:DD.F/subsystem
A link to the bus,
.Pa ../../../bus/pci .
.It Pa /sys/devices/pci0000:00/0000:BB:DD.F/config
The function's configuration space, read-only, as Linux gives it: 4096
bytes when the ECAM window of the MCFG reaches the function's extended
space, else 256, each dword read as the function answers it at the time
of the read; to a process without privilege only the first 64, the
header, since a few devices act on a read of their other registers.
A driver that stands in for another chip may report its identity, which
then replaces the IDs, revision, class and subsystem IDs of the header
and of the files above; a reported function with no device behind it has
a header of zeros besides.
Its size in
.Xr stat 2
is 4096, as every file here has.
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:BB:DD.F/iommu_group
On the x86-64 kernel, a link to the function's IOMMU group under
.Pa /sys/kernel/iommu_groups ,
for a function an IOMMU unit translates; see
.Xr vfio 4 .
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:BB:DD.F/driver_override
The driver that alone may take the function, or
.Ql (null)
for none, as on Linux.
Root writes a name of up to 31 bytes, less a newline, or an empty line to
clear it; a longer name is
.Er EINVAL .
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/devices/0000:BB:DD.F/driver
A link to
.Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/vfio-pci
while the function is bound to it.
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/vfio-pci/{bind,unbind}
Write-only, mode 0200.
A function's name written to
.Pa bind
gives it to vfio-pci when its
.Pa driver_override
names vfio-pci, or it has none and its ids were given to
.Pa new_id ;
otherwise, or for a name no function has, the write is
.Er ENODEV .
It is
.Er EBUSY
while a driver holds the function and
.Er EINVAL
for a bridge.
A name written to
.Pa unbind
takes the function back, and is
.Er EBUSY
while a program has it open.
The directory has a link, named as the function, to each function bound.
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/vfio-pci/{new_id,remove_id}
Write-only, mode 0200.
.Ql VENDOR DEVICE
in hexadecimal written to
.Pa new_id
adds the ids vfio-pci matches, at most 16, and binds every function of
them that has no driver;
.Pa remove_id
forgets ids it gave, and is
.Er ENODEV
for others.
.It Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers_probe
Write-only, mode 0200: a function's name written here gives it to
vfio-pci if it has no driver and vfio-pci matches it, and leaves it alone
otherwise.
.It Pa /sys/kernel/iommu_groups/N
On the x86-64 kernel, IOMMU group N, which
.Xr vfio 4
gives as
.Pa /dev/vfio/N .
.Pa type
reads
.Ql identity ;
.Pa reserved_regions
has a line for each range the group's functions may not map, its start
and end as
.Ql 0x
and sixteen hexadecimal digits and its kind,
.Ql direct ,
.Ql direct-relaxable
or
.Ql msi ,
sorted by start, as Linux writes them; and
.Pa devices
has a link to each function of the group.
The directory is there with no group when no IOMMU unit translates.
.It Pa /sys/bus/usb/devices/B-P[.P...]
A symbolic link for each running USB device, named by its bus, the number
of its controller from 1, and its port on the controller and on each hub
below, to
.Pa ../../../devices/usb/B-P[.P...] .
.It Pa /sys/devices/usb/B-P[.P...]/uevent
The device's variables:
.Ql PRODUCT ,
its vendor, product and release in lowercase hexadecimal, separated by
slashes;
.Ql BUSNUM
and
.Ql DEVNUM ,
its bus and the controller's slot for it, three decimal digits each; and
.Ql DEVTYPE=usb_device .
A write of an action sends that uevent.
.It Pa /sys/devices/usb/B-P[.P...]/B-P[.P...]:C.I
The interface of a USB keyboard or mouse, by its configuration value C
and its number I, made with its input device and linked from
.Pa /sys/bus/usb/devices :
.Pa bInterfaceNumber ,
.Pa bAlternateSetting ,
.Pa bInterfaceClass ,
.Pa bInterfaceSubClass
and
.Pa bInterfaceProtocol ,
as Linux formats them;
.Pa modalias ,
.Ql usb:v...ic03isc01ip01in00
as Linux writes it;
a
.Pa subsystem
link to
.Pa /sys/bus/usb ;
and its
.Pa uevent :
.Ql DEVTYPE=usb_interface ,
.Ql DRIVER ,
.Ql usbhid ,
.Ql PRODUCT ,
.Ql TYPE ,
.Ql INTERFACE ,
its class, subclass and protocol in decimal, and
.Ql MODALIAS .
.It Pa /sys/class/drm
A link for each DRM node and connector to its directory below its
device, and
.Pa version ,
.Ql drm 1.1.0 20060810 ,
Linux's.
.It Pa /sys/devices/.../drm/cardN , /sys/devices/.../drm/renderDN
A DRM device's card node, and its render node when its driver renders,
below the device's directory: the PCI function's, or
.Pa /sys/devices/platform/NAME
for a device with no PCI function, such as vkms.
Each has
.Pa dev ,
its numbers as
.Ql 226:N ;
.Pa uevent ,
.Ql MAJOR ,
.Ql MINOR ,
.Ql DEVNAME
.Pq Pa dri/cardN
and
.Ql DEVTYPE=drm_minor ;
a
.Pa subsystem
link to
.Pa /sys/class/drm ;
and a
.Pa device
link to the device's directory.
.It Pa /sys/devices/.../drm/cardN/cardN-CONNECTOR
A connector of the card, by its name, such as
.Pa card0-Virtual-1 :
.Pa status ,
.Ql connected ,
.Ql disconnected
or
.Ql unknown ,
which takes a write of
.Ql detect ,
.Ql on ,
.Ql on-digital
or
.Ql off ,
with one newline at most, to force it; when the force changes, or the write
is
.Ql detect ,
the connector is probed again at once, and when the force changes a
hotplug event follows;
.Pa enabled ,
.Ql enabled
while an encoder drives it;
.Pa dpms ,
.Ql On
while its CRTC is active and
.Ql Off
else;
.Pa modes ,
the name of each mode a line;
.Pa edid ,
the sink's EDID bytes;
.Pa connector_id ;
its
.Pa uevent
with
.Ql DEVTYPE=drm_connector ;
and a
.Pa device
link to the card.
.It Pa /sys/dev/char/226:N
A link for each DRM node, by its numbers, to its directory; libdrm reads
.Pa device/subsystem ,
.Pa device/uevent ,
the identity files and
.Pa device/drm
through it.
.It Pa /sys/devices/platform/NAME
A platform device's directory: its
.Pa uevent ,
.Ql DRIVER
and
.Ql MODALIAS=platform:NAME ,
a
.Pa subsystem
link to
.Pa /sys/bus/platform ,
and its
.Pa drm .
.Pa /sys/bus/platform/devices
links each.
.It Pa /sys/devices/platform/i8042/serioK
The PS/2 controller's port K, 0 for the keyboard's and 1 for the mouse's,
and serio2 below serio1, a Synaptics touchpad's pass-through port, as
Linux numbers them, each linked from
.Pa /sys/bus/serio/devices
once a device on it registers:
.Pa description ,
.Ql i8042 KBD port ,
.Ql i8042 AUX port
or
.Ql Synaptics pass-through ;
.Pa modalias ,
.Ql serio:ty06pr00id00ex00
for the keyboard's translated port,
.Ql serio:ty01pr00id00ex00
for the mouse's and
.Ql serio:ty05pr00id00ex00
for the pass-through port;
.Pa bind_mode ,
.Ql auto ;
.Pa firmware_id ,
the device's ACPI PNP IDs as Linux's i8042 writes them,
.Ql PNP: LEN0036 PNP0f13
or the like, or empty;
.Pa id/type ,
.Pa id/proto ,
.Pa id/id
and
.Pa id/extra ,
two hexadecimal digits each;
a
.Pa subsystem
link to
.Pa /sys/bus/serio ;
and its
.Pa uevent ,
.Ql DRIVER ,
.Ql atkbd
or
.Ql psmouse ,
the four
.Ql SERIO_
variables,
.Ql MODALIAS
and, for a port with PNP IDs,
.Ql SERIO_FIRMWARE_ID .
A TrackPoint's port has its settings beside, as Linux's trackpoint.c makes
them, mode 0644:
.Pa sensitivity ,
.Pa speed ,
.Pa inertia ,
.Pa reach ,
.Pa draghys ,
.Pa mindrag ,
.Pa thresh ,
.Pa upthresh ,
.Pa ztime ,
.Pa jenks ,
.Pa drift_time ,
.Pa press_to_select ,
.Pa skipback
and
.Pa ext_dev
for an IBM stick, and
.Pa sensitivity
and
.Pa press_to_select
alone for the others; see
.Xr input 4 .
The i8042's own
.Pa uevent
has
.Ql DRIVER=i8042
and
.Ql MODALIAS=platform:i8042 .
.It Pa /sys/class/input
A link for each input device,
.Pa inputN ,
and for its event device,
.Pa eventN ,
to its directory.
An input device's N is the lowest free from 0, as Linux numbers them;
an event device's is the N of
.Pa /dev/input/eventN
.Pq Xr input 4 .
.It Pa /sys/devices/.../input/inputN
An input device, below its parent: the i8042's port, the USB interface,
or
.Pa /sys/devices/virtual
for a device with neither, such as the ACPI buttons.
It has
.Pa name
and
.Pa phys ,
the strings of EVIOCGNAME and EVIOCGPHYS;
.Pa uniq ,
empty, since no driver gives a serial number;
.Pa properties
and
.Pa capabilities/ev ,
.Pa key ,
.Pa rel ,
.Pa abs ,
.Pa msc ,
.Pa led ,
.Pa snd ,
.Pa ff
and
.Pa sw ,
bitmaps as Linux prints them, in hexadecimal words of the caller's long,
the highest word that has a bit set first;
.Pa id/bustype ,
.Pa id/vendor ,
.Pa id/product
and
.Pa id/version ,
four hexadecimal digits each;
.Pa modalias ,
.Ql input:b...v...p...e...-e...
with each kind of event the device sends;
a
.Pa subsystem
link to
.Pa /sys/class/input ;
a
.Pa device
link to its parent, but under
.Pa /sys/devices/virtual ;
and its
.Pa uevent :
.Ql PRODUCT ,
.Ql NAME
and
.Ql PHYS
when it has them,
.Ql PROP ,
.Ql EV ,
a variable for each other kind of event it sends, and
.Ql MODALIAS ,
cut short with
.Ql +,
as Linux cuts it when the message would pass 2048 bytes.
.It Pa /sys/devices/.../input/inputN/eventN
The input device's event device:
.Pa dev ,
.Ql 13:M ,
where M is 64 more than N;
.Pa uevent ,
.Ql MAJOR ,
.Ql MINOR
and
.Ql DEVNAME
.Pq Pa input/eventN ;
a
.Pa subsystem
link to
.Pa /sys/class/input ;
and a
.Pa device
link to the input device.
.It Pa /sys/dev/char/13:M
A link for each event device, by its numbers, to its directory.
.It Pa /sys/kernel/debug
The directory where the kernel mounts debugfs; see
.Xr debugfs 5 .
.It Pa /sys/kernel/uevent_seqnum
The number of the last uevent sent.
.It Pa /sys/class/net/IF
A directory for each network interface, named as
.Xr ip 8
names it, with Linux's attribute files, one value and a newline each:
.Pa address
and
.Pa broadcast ,
the hardware addresses in lowercase;
.Pa addr_len ,
6;
.Pa carrier ,
1 while the interface is up and its card has a link;
.Pa dev_id ,
0x0;
.Pa duplex ,
.Ql full ,
.Ql half
or
.Ql unknown ;
.Pa flags ,
0x1003 up and 0x1002 down;
.Pa ifindex
and
.Pa iflink ,
the interface's index;
.Pa mtu ,
the interface's MTU, 1500 unless set lower;
.Pa operstate ,
.Ql up ,
.Ql down
or
.Ql unknown ;
.Pa speed ,
the link's megabits a second, -1 without a link;
.Pa tx_queue_len ,
1000;
.Pa type ,
1 for Ethernet; and
.Pa uevent ,
.Ql INTERFACE
and
.Ql IFINDEX .
.It Pa /sys/class/net/IF/statistics
The interface's counters:
.Pa rx_bytes ,
.Pa rx_packets ,
.Pa rx_errors ,
.Pa rx_dropped ,
.Pa tx_bytes ,
.Pa tx_packets ,
.Pa tx_errors ,
.Pa tx_dropped ,
.Pa multicast
and
.Pa collisions ,
those
.Pa /proc/net/dev
gives.
.It Pa /sys/class/net/IF/device
A symbolic link to the interface's PCI function,
.Pa ../../../devices/pci0000:00/0000:BB:DD.F .
.It Pa /sys/class/ieee80211/phyN
A registered radio's attributes and driver links; see
.Xr wlan 4 .
.It Pa /sys/class/net/wlanN/phy80211
A link to the radio of a wireless station interface.
.It Pa /sys/class/rtc/rtc0
The CMOS clock of
.Xr rtc 4 :
.Pa date
and
.Pa time ,
in UTC;
.Pa since_epoch ,
its seconds since 1970;
.Pa name ,
.Ql rtc_cmos ;
.Pa hctosys ,
1, since the boot set the system's time from it; and
.Pa wakealarm ,
the wake alarm in seconds since 1970, empty when none is set, which takes a
write of seconds since 1970, of
.Ql + Ns Ar N
for
.Ar N
seconds from now, or of 0 to clear it, and a new time while one is set is
.Er EBUSY ,
as on Linux.
.It Pa /sys/class/rtc/rtc0/device/power/wakeup
.Ql enabled :
the clock's alarm may wake the machine.
.It Pa /sys/class/backlight/NAME
A backlight of the panel, named by its driver, such as
.Pa intel_backlight ,
one for each backlight a driver registered:
.Pa max_brightness ,
the highest level;
.Pa brightness ,
the level set, which takes a write of a level from 0 to
.Pa max_brightness ;
.Pa actual_brightness ,
the level the hardware has;
.Pa bl_power ,
0 while root leaves the backlight on and 4 once root turns it off, which
takes a write of either; the backlight is off while the console is blank
too, and
.Pa bl_power
still says what root wrote; and
.Pa type ,
.Ql raw ,
a level of the hardware's own.
Another value is
.Er EINVAL .
See
.Xr backlight 4 .
.It Pa /sys/power/state
The sleep states,
.Ql freeze mem .
A write of either suspends to idle: every process but the writer is held,
the processor idles, and they go on at a wake event, the clock's alarm or
a key, when the write returns.
The Atheros Wi-Fi station stops before the freeze and starts again after
it if it was running and rfkill permits it; its association is cleared.
Another state is
.Er EINVAL .
.It Pa /sys/power/mem_sleep
.Ql [s2idle] :
.Ql mem
is suspend to idle.
.It Pa /sys/devices/system/cpu
The processors:
.Pa online ,
.Pa possible
and
.Pa present ,
each the processors started, as Linux lists a set of them, such as
.Ql 0-3 ;
.Pa offline ,
empty;
.Pa kernel_max ,
.Ql 63 ,
the highest number a processor may have; and a directory
.Pa cpuN
for each processor started.
.It Pa /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpuN/topology
Where the processor sits, which
.Xr lscpu 1
reads:
.Pa physical_package_id
and
.Pa core_id ,
its package and its core in the package, as
.Pa /proc/cpuinfo
gives them;
.Pa die_id ,
.Ql 0 ;
.Pa thread_siblings
and
.Pa core_cpus ,
the processors started on its core, and
.Pa core_siblings ,
.Pa package_cpus
and
.Pa die_cpus ,
those in its package, each a hexadecimal mask such as
.Ql 3 ,
and the same as a list in the file of each name with
.Pa _list
after it.
A cluster is a core:
.Pa cluster_id
is
.Pa core_id
and
.Pa cluster_cpus
is
.Pa core_cpus .
glibc's
.Xr sysconf 3
counts the processors that are online from
.Pa online .
.It Pa /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpuN/cache
A directory
.Pa indexM
for each of the processor's caches, M from 0, as Linux's
.Pa cacheinfo
describes them from
.Fn cpuid :
leaf 4 on Intel and on every vendor but AMD and Hygon, leaf 0x8000001D on
those two when it is there, and otherwise AMD's leaves 0x80000005 and
0x80000006, which give an L1 data, an L1 instruction, an L2 and an L3
cache, the last only when the processor has one.
A processor that describes no cache has no
.Pa cache .
Wine reads these for
.Fn GetLogicalProcessorInformation .
.It Pa /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpuN/cache/indexM
A cache:
.Pa level ;
.Pa type ,
.Ql Data ,
.Ql Instruction
or
.Ql Unified ;
.Pa size ,
in KiB with a
.Ql K
after it, such as
.Ql 32K ;
.Pa coherency_line_size ,
the line in bytes;
.Pa ways_of_associativity ;
.Pa number_of_sets ;
.Pa physical_line_partition ,
the lines to a tag;
.Pa id ,
the APIC ID above the bits that number the logical processors that share
it; and
.Pa shared_cpu_map
and
.Pa shared_cpu_list ,
the processors started that share it, as a mask and as a list.
Two processors share a cache whose leaf says that several share it when
their APIC IDs agree above those bits; on AMD's older leaves the L3 is
shared by the package, and every other cache is the processor's own.
The figures are Linux's, from the same leaves, in Linux's text.
.It Pa /sys/devices/system/node
The NUMA nodes, of which there is one:
.Pa possible ,
.Pa online ,
.Pa has_cpu ,
.Pa has_memory
and
.Pa has_normal_memory ,
each
.Ql 0 ,
and its directory
.Pa node0 .
.It Pa /sys/devices/system/node/node0
The one node, which has every processor and all the memory:
.Pa cpumap
and
.Pa cpulist ,
the processors started as a mask and as a list, and
.Pa distance ,
.Ql 10 ,
its distance from itself.
.It Pa /sys/devices/system/clocksource/clocksource0
.Pa current_clocksource ,
the counter that keeps the clock, by Linux's name:
.Ql tsc ,
.Ql hpet ,
.Ql acpi_pm ,
or
.Ql jiffies
when the tick alone keeps it; and
.Pa available_clocksource ,
the counters there are, best first as Linux rates them, each followed by
a space.
Neither may be written.
.It Pa /sys/class/dmi/id
The firmware's SMBIOS fields, a file for each that its table holds, as
Linux's DMI code reads them:
.Pa bios_vendor ,
.Pa bios_version ,
.Pa bios_date
and
.Pa bios_release
from the BIOS Information structure, with
.Pa ec_firmware_release ;
.Pa sys_vendor ,
.Pa product_name ,
.Pa product_version ,
.Pa product_serial ,
.Pa product_uuid ,
.Pa product_sku
and
.Pa product_family
from the System Information structure;
.Pa board_vendor ,
.Pa board_name ,
.Pa board_version ,
.Pa board_serial
and
.Pa board_asset_tag
from the Base Board Information structure; and
.Pa chassis_vendor ,
.Pa chassis_type ,
.Pa chassis_version ,
.Pa chassis_serial
and
.Pa chassis_asset_tag
from the Chassis Information structure.
Each is taken from the first structure of its type long enough to hold
it.
A string that the structure does not name, or one of spaces alone, is
empty; a release is
.Ql MAJOR.MINOR ,
and absent when the firmware gives 0xFF for both; the chassis type is
in decimal; the UUID is in lowercase with its dashes, with its first
three fields' bytes reversed from SMBIOS 2.6 on, and absent when it is
all zeros or all ones.
The serial numbers and the UUID have mode 0400, and are
.Er EACCES
to a caller that is not privileged.
.Pa modalias
is
.Ql dmi: ,
then for each field of Linux's list the table holds a short prefix and
the field with its spaces, its colons and its bytes past ASCII taken out,
each after a colon, and a colon at the end, as Linux makes it for
udev's hardware database.
Wine reads these for its own SMBIOS table.
On Linux
.Pa id
is a link to
.Pa /sys/devices/virtual/dmi/id ;
here it is the directory itself.
.It Pa /sys/devices/system/memory
The memory blocks, which
.Xr lsmem 1
reads:
.Pa block_size_bytes ,
the size of all the memory the kernel manages in hexadecimal bytes, and
one block,
.Pa memory0 ,
whose
.Pa state
is
.Ql online ,
.Pa online
.Ql 1 ,
.Pa removable
.Ql 0
and
.Pa phys_index
.Ql 00000000 .
Linux divides memory into blocks of 128 MiB or more and gives each its
own directory; the one block here has the size of the memory the kernel
manages and is given at address 0, though that memory has holes and need
not start there.
.It Pa /sys/block/DISK
A directory for each disk of
.Pa /dev ,
and each loop device with a file attached, by its node's name, which
.Ic lsblk
reads: its
.Pa dev ,
the node's numbers as
.Ar MAJOR : Ns Ar MINOR ;
.Pa size
in 512-byte sectors;
.Pa removable ,
1 for a USB stick;
.Pa ro ,
.Pa range ,
.Pa ext_range ,
.Pa capability ,
.Pa hidden
and
.Pa alignment_offset
as Linux has them for a disk; and its
.Pa uevent
with the numbers, the name and
.Ql DEVTYPE=disk .
.It Pa /sys/block/DISK/PART
A directory for each partition published, with
.Pa dev ,
.Pa size ,
.Pa start ,
its first sector,
.Pa partition ,
its number,
.Pa ro ,
.Pa alignment_offset
and its
.Pa uevent .
.It Pa /sys/block/DISK/queue
The disk's queue: sectors of 512 bytes, transfers of at most 128 KiB, no
discard, a rotating disk, and no scheduler.
.It Pa /sys/block/DISK/device
Its
.Pa model
and
.Pa serial ,
from the drive's identity, and its
.Pa vendor ,
.Ql ATA ,
or
.Ql USB
for a stick.
.It Pa /sys/block/loopN/loop
A loop device's, while a file backs it, as on Linux:
.Pa backing_file ,
the path the file was attached by,
.Pa autoclear ,
and
.Pa offset ,
.Pa sizelimit ,
.Pa partscan
and
.Pa dio ,
each 0.
util-linux's
.Xr losetup 8
reads them, since the release
.Xr uname 2
gives is a Linux one.
.It Pa /sys/dev/block/MAJOR:MINOR
A link for each disk node to its directory under
.Pa /sys/block .
.El
.Pp
A
.Pa uevent
reports a size of 4096 bytes, as Linux's attribute files do, and reads as
long as its text.
.Pp
A driver adds its own files, directories and links below these
directories and below each other, from its own source; a link reads
relative to itself, as on Linux.
.Sh UEVENTS
The kernel sends a uevent to the sockets of
.Dv NETLINK_KOBJECT_UEVENT
bound to group 1
.Pq Xr netlink 7
when a DRM node or connector comes or goes, and an input device, its
event device, its USB interface or the i8042's port,
.Ql add
or
.Ql remove ,
and
.Ql change
with
.Ql HOTPLUG=1
on the card when its outputs change.
A message is
.Ql ACTION@DEVPATH
and the variables
.Ql ACTION ,
.Ql DEVPATH ,
the directory's path less
.Pa /sys ,
.Ql SUBSYSTEM ,
those of the directory's
.Pa uevent
and
.Ql SEQNUM ,
each ended by a NUL, the variables 2048 bytes at most, as Linux's
kobject_uevent_env sends it.
.Pp
Root's write to a device's
.Pa uevent
of an action,
.Ql add ,
.Ql remove ,
.Ql change ,
.Ql move ,
.Ql online ,
.Ql offline ,
.Ql bind
or
.Ql unbind ,
sends that uevent, with
.Ql SYNTH_UUID=0 ,
as udev's coldplug writes it; an action followed by a UUID of 36
characters and
.Ql KEY=value
words sends
.Ql SYNTH_UUID
and a
.Ql SYNTH_ARG_KEY=value
for each.
Another word, a UUID of another length or a word without
.Ql =
is
.Er EINVAL .
.Sh LIMITS
Linux's tree has much more: the other device classes, the attribute files
beside a USB device's
.Pa uevent ,
the processors' frequency, power and microcode files, and the firmware
interfaces but the DMI fields.
Rach2 has none of them.
A cache's, a node's, the clock source's and the DMI directory have no
.Pa uevent ,
.Pa power
or
.Pa subsystem ;
a node has no
.Pa meminfo ,
.Pa numastat
or
.Pa vmstat ;
.Pa clocksource0
has no
.Pa unbind_clocksource ,
and its counter cannot be changed.
Linux lists the HPET and the ACPI PM timer when the machine has both;
here only the one the kernel found is listed.
A DMI string is cut at 63 bytes.
.Pa /sys/dev/char
lists only the DRM nodes and the event devices.
A virtio-gpu card's
.Pa device
leads to its PCI function, where Linux puts a
.Pa virtio0
directory between them.
Linux's
.Pa /sys/block
holds a link for each disk into its device's directory, which has a
.Pa stat ,
.Pa holders ,
.Pa slaves
and more; here it holds the disk's directory itself, with the files above.
Linux lists every loop device it has made; here a loop device is listed
only while a file is attached to it.
Linux suspends to idle with the devices powered down and its clocks of
time since boot stopped; here the Atheros Wi-Fi station stops, the other
devices stay up, interrupts are taken, and the clocks count on.
Linux's
.Pa /sys/class/net
holds a link for each interface into its device's directory; here it holds
the interface's directory itself.
.Pa /sys/class/net/lo
has Linux's loopback type, 772, flags, 0x9, and MTU, 65536, and no
.Pa device .
Where Linux nests a PCI function under the bridge it is behind, and a USB
device under its controller's function, they are side by side here under
.Pa pci0000:00
and
.Pa usb ,
the links lead there, and the root hubs are not listed.
A USB device's
.Pa uevent
leaves out the device node's numbers and name, since no node of the
device exists, and puts
.Ql DEVTYPE
last, since
.Ic lsusb
takes a first line of
.Ql DEVTYPE
for an interface's.
Only a USB keyboard's or mouse's interface is listed, and only while its
input device is registered; its
.Pa uevent
gives the device's class as 0/0/0, which a boot keyboard and mouse have.
An input device has no
.Pa power ,
.Pa inhibited
or
.Pa driver ,
and
.Pa /sys/bus/serio/drivers
is empty.
The entries of an input device that a driver registers in an interrupt,
as the USB stack does, are made by the kernel thread kinputd a moment
later, where Linux makes them at once.
